</style>

<div class="panel panel-info debriefing-page">
    <div class="panel-heading">
        <h1 class="panel-title" style="font-size:2em">Deception Debriefing Form</h1>
    </div>
    <div class="panel-body">
        <div class="debriefing-content">
            <h2>The City University of New York</h2>
            <h2>Debriefing Form for Participation in a Research Study</h2>

            <div class="debriefing-meta">
                <p><strong>Title of Research Study:</strong> Student Teacher Judgements about Child Learner Traits</p>
                <p><strong>Principal Investigator:</strong> Anna Workman, M.Phil.<br>Doctoral Candidate, Clinical Psychology<br>Department of Psychology<br>The City College of the City University of New York</p>
                <p><strong>Faculty Advisor:</strong> Sarah O&rsquo;Neill, Ph.D.<br>Associate Professor<br>Department of Psychology<br>The City College of the City University of New York</p>
                <p><strong>Research Sponsor:</strong> Colin Powell Graduate Fellowship</p>
                <hr class="debriefing-meta-rule">
            </div>

            <p>Thank you for your participation in our study! Your participation is greatly appreciated.</p>

            <h3>Purpose</h3>
            <ul class="debriefing-list">
                <li>Earlier in our consent form we informed you that the purpose of the study was to understand how students studying education make judgments about child learner traits.</li>
                <li>This is true, but we&rsquo;re particularly interested in how fast you make these judgments, and whether the speed of your responses tells us something about mental associations your brain has made between concepts. These associations happen automatically, before you&rsquo;ve had time to think.</li>
                <li>Our brain is constantly building shortcuts. When we have seen two things paired together often enough (say, &quot;flowers&quot; and &quot;pleasant&quot;), our brain links those things together. The computer task you completed is a reaction-time task that is used to measure what concepts your brain has learned to link together, even when you&rsquo;re not consciously aware of it.</li>
                <li>Our study was developing a new version of this computer task to understand how students studying education make judgements about child learner traits, and whether those judgements differ depending on children&rsquo;s racial identity. We wanted to see whether photographs of Asian American children were more likely to be paired with positive learner traits, like smart or hard-working.</li>
                <li>It is important to know that the computer task measures associations, or patterns that we have learned over time. The computer task does not measure character or intentions. These associations are shaped by things we are exposed to in our culture. It can also be the case that these unconscious associations conflict with an individual&rsquo;s explicit values.</li>
                <li>Unfortunately, in order to properly test our hypothesis, we could not provide you with all of these details prior to your participation. This ensured that your reactions in this study were spontaneous and not influenced by prior knowledge about the purpose of the study. If we had told you the actual purposes of our study, your ability to complete the computer task could have been affected. We regret the need to keep these details from you, but we hope you understand the reason for it.</li>
                <li>It is important to remember that the computer task is a group-level research tool. Individual scores are not informative and a single score does not predict how somebody will behave.</li>
            </ul>

            <h3>Confidentiality</h3>
            <ul class="debriefing-list">
                <li>Please note that although the purpose of this study has changed from the originally stated purpose, everything else on the consent form is correct. This includes the ways in which we will keep your data confidential. We will securely store research records and only researchers will have access to the records.</li>
                <li>Please do not disclose research procedures and/or hypotheses to anyone who might participate in this study in the future as this could affect the results of the study.</li>
            </ul>

            <h3>Option to Remove Your Data</h3>
            <ul class="debriefing-list">
                <li>Now that you know the true purpose of our study and are fully informed, you may decide that you do not want your data used in this research. If you would like your data removed from the study and permanently deleted, please email Anna Workman or Dr. Sarah O&rsquo;Neill (see below for their contact information).</li>
            </ul>

            <h3>Payment for Participation</h3>
            <ul class="debriefing-list">
                <li>Whether you agree or do not agree to have your data used for this study, you will still receive the following compensation for your participation: You will have a one in thirty chance of winning the raffle prize. If you win the raffle prize, you will receive a $20 Amazon gift card via email within one week of winning. The gift card will be emailed to the address you provide.</li>
            </ul>

            <h3>Final Report</h3>
            <ul class="debriefing-list">
                <li>If you would like to receive a copy of the final report of this study (or a summary of the findings) when it is completed, please feel free to contact us.</li>
            </ul>

            <h3>Questions, Comments or Concerns</h3>
            <p>If you have any questions, comments or concerns about the research, you can talk to:</p>
            <ul class="debriefing-contact-list">
                <li>Anna Workman, M.Phil, <a href="mailto:anna.workman93@stu-mail.ccny.cuny.edu">anna.workman93@stu-mail.ccny.cuny.edu</a></li>
                <li>Sarah O&rsquo;Neill, Ph.D., <a href="mailto:soneill2@ccny.cuny.edu">soneill2@ccny.cuny.edu</a></li>
            </ul>
            <p>If you have questions about your rights as a research participant, or you have comments or concerns that you would like to discuss with someone other than the researchers, please call the CUNY Research Compliance Administrator at 646-664-8918 or email <a href="mailto:HRPP@cuny.edu">HRPP@cuny.edu</a>.</p>

            <h3>Further Reading(s)</h3>
            <ul class="debriefing-list">
                <li>
                    If you would like to learn more about measures of implicit bias or ADHD in Asian Americans, please see the following references:
                    <ul class="debriefing-sublist">
                        <li>Feng, A., O&rsquo;Neill, S., &amp; Rostain, A. L. (2024). Contributors to Underdiagnosis of ADHD among Asian Americans: A Narrative Review. <em>Journal of Attention Disorders, 28</em>(12), 1499-1519. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1177/10870547241264113" target="_blank" rel="noopener">https://doi.org/10.1177/10870547241264113</a></li>
                        <li>Greenwald, A. G., &amp; Banaji, M. R. (1995). Implicit social cognition: attitudes, self-esteem, and stereotypes. <em>Psychological Review, 102</em>(1), 4-27. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1037/0033-295x.102.1.4" target="_blank" rel="noopener">https://doi.org/10.1037/0033-295x.102.1.4</a></li>
                    </ul>
                </li>
            </ul>

            <p>Please print a copy of this debriefing form and keep it for your records. Once again, thank you for your participation in this study!</p>
        </div>

        <div class="debriefing-actions">
            <button pi-message-done type="button" class="btn btn-primary btn-lg">
                Finish
            </button>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
